huawei the world's largest maker of  telecom equipment became a global leader  in designing cutting-edge chips rivaling  those made by samsung apple and nvidia  but ever since the us imposed sanctions  against huawei in 2019 the chinese tech  giant has been losing its foothold in  the global smartphone market  the restrictions that came down on  huawei in 2019 certainly took the  company by surprise the u.s for a long  time saw the company as a national  security threat but it took quite a few  years for washington to kind of really  mobilize itself and take some serious  action against the company a spike in  demand has contributed to the global  chip shortage but ongoing u.s china  political frictions have exacerbated the  situation for huawei so what led the us  to impose restrictions on the chinese  tech giant  first the u.s has long seen huawei as a  national security threat especially as  it was advancing so quickly in 5g  technology this was seen as a problem  among some us policy makers who have  long been concerned that huawei  telecommunications gear can in some way  be intercepted or tapped by the chinese  government huawei has denied this  allegation  two more reasons for the restrictions  allegations of intellectual property  theft and violation of trade sanctions  in 2019 the u.s charged huawei with  attempting to steal trade secrets from  t-mobile and with violating trade  sanctions against iran  the alleged fraudulent financial schemes  used by huawei and its chief financial  officer were not just illegal but  detrimental to the security of the  united states huawei previously denied  all of these allegations but in  september the company's cfo reached a  deal with the us admitting to some  wrongdoing in the iran trade sanctions  case  both cases are still ongoing  later in 2019 the u.s placed huawei on  what's called the entity list banning  u.s companies like google and qualcomm  from working with huawei  then in 2020 the us tightened the terms  of the entity listing twice cutting  huawei off from its own chip  manufacturers and then later blocking  huawei from accessing any chip built  using us technology us technology is so  deeply embedded in this supply chain so  this was a truly destructive measure  that blocked the company from chip  makers not just in the u.s but in taiwan  in europe even in mainland china despite  the restrictions any company that wants  to sell to huawei can apply for a  license from the us government but these  are often difficult to get for example  qualcomm is able to sell 4g chip  technology to huawei other companies are  selling more rudimentary chips to huawei  and you can see that that's really an  offshoot of this underlying desire and  policy circles in washington to reign in  huawei's 5g technology without totally  sort of choking off the company from any  components whatsoever  since september 2020 huawei has been  relying on its stockpile of chips but  its supply is now running low huawei is  investing in domestic chip makers but it  could still take them years to catch up  to us technology huawei released a new  phone in july but without high-tech  chips it can't connect to 5g networks  and analysts say it will struggle to  compete globally in september huawei  deputy chairman eric shu said the  company's smartphone revenue will drop  by up to 40 billion dollars this year  and so far there haven't been any signs  from washington that the us will loosen  these restrictions you're seeing huawei  pull back on 5g you're seeing huawei  lose out on 5g contracts you're seeing  its 5g phone sales totally collapsed and  in many ways this was the goal of some  of the sanctions  [Music]
 

